changelog

Version 3.0 — W3C Verifiable Credentials format

This schema version is for W3C Verifiable Credential (VC) format documents ONLY.
It follows the W3C Verifiable Credentials Data Model 2.0 and is meant to be
issued / signed / verified with the TrustVC library (@trustvc/trustvc).

What changed from 2.2:
- The document is now a W3C Verifiable Credential. The top level carries the
  standard VC envelope: "@context", "type" (must include "VerifiableCredential"),
  "issuer", "validFrom" (replaces "issuedOn"), "validUntil" (replaces "expiresOn")
  and "credentialSubject".
- All transcript data (name, description, recipient, admissionDate,
  graduationDate, attainmentDate, qualificationLevel, fieldOfStudy, transcript,
  cumulativeScore, skills, additionalData) now lives inside "credentialSubject"
  instead of at the document root.
- The issuer is now the single W3C "issuer" property (a DID/URL string, or an
  object with an "id"), replacing the OpenAttestation "issuers" array.
- The issuer keeps the contact fields the OpenAttestation issuers[] carried:
  "name" (required, alongside "id"), plus optional "url", "email" and "phone".
  The old "did" is now "issuer.id". The OpenAttestation-only fields
  "documentStore" and "certificateStore" are gone.
- "identityProof" is replaced by "issuer.id". The DID *is* the identity proof:
  a DNS-TXT / DNS-DID identityProof bound an issuer to a domain, and did:web
  does the same thing by putting the domain in the DID and resolving the DID
  document over HTTPS from it; a DID-type identityProof named the issuer's
  public key, which is now the verificationMethod in that DID document (or,
  for did:key, the DID itself). The binding moved from a DNS TXT record to the
  DID method; it did not go away.
  The W3C VC v2 context marks "issuer" as @protected, so no scoped @context can
  be attached to it; context.json declares these as top-level terms instead.
- The OpenAttestation "$template" / "network" fields are removed. Use the W3C/
  TrustVC "renderMethod" for rendering and "credentialStatus" for revocation.
  "renderMethod" entries require "id", "type" ("EMBEDDED_RENDERER") and
  "templateName" — the same three fields "$template" required as url / type /
  name, so a malformed renderer is still caught at validation time.
  "credentialStatus" is a W3C VC Data Model 2.0 Bitstring Status List entry and
  requires "type" ("BitstringStatusListEntry"), "statusPurpose" ("revocation"
  or "suspension"), "statusListIndex" and "statusListCredential". Only the
  W3C VC 2.0 status type is accepted here.
- Optional W3C/TrustVC properties are supported: "validUntil", "credentialStatus",
  "credentialSchema", "renderMethod", "id" and "proof".

Required fields — unchanged in substance from 2.2:
- The document requires "@context", "type", "issuer", "validFrom" and
  "credentialSubject". "validFrom" carries over the requirement that 2.2
  placed on "issuedOn".
- The credential "id" is NOT supplied by the issuer. 2.2 required an "id" at the
  document root; in a W3C VC the "id" is generated — TrustVC mints a urn:uuid
  while signing and rejects any document that already carries one. The schema
  enforces this: an unsigned credential (no "proof") must not have an "id", a
  signed one may. An issuer's own reference number therefore no longer belongs
  in "id".
- "credentialSubject" requires "type", "name" and "recipient". "transcript"
  is optional, exactly as it was in 1.0 through 2.2. No field that was
  optional in 2.2 has become mandatory.
- "credentialSubject.type" must name the "Transcript" type. It is not merely
  descriptive: context.json defines every term inside that type-scoped
  @context, so a subject without it cannot be signed.

Extra properties:
- "recipient" and the "transcript[]" items accept extra properties, as they
  did in 2.2. Issuers use them widely (for example a recipient's date of
  birth, or a course row's semester), and their meanings differ between
  issuers, so they are not defined in context.json.
- An extra property must be defined in a context before the credential can
  be signed. A W3C VC is canonicalised as an RDF graph, so a property no
  context defines has no IRI and signing fails ("Safe mode validation
  error"). The issuer hosts its own context defining its fields and lists it
  in "@context" after this version's context. That context must never change
  once used, and it can only add new terms, not redefine OpenCerts or VC ones.
  JSON Schema cannot check this, so schema.json accepts such fields either way.
- Every other object the schema describes sets "additionalProperties": false:
  "credentialSubject" (matching the "additionalProperties": false 2.2 had on
  its root), "issuer", and the "skills[]", "qualificationLevel[]" and
  "fieldOfStudy[]" items.
- Freeform content belongs in "additionalData", which is "@type": "@json",
  accepts arbitrary nested content and needs no context.
- The document root stays open: it is the W3C VC envelope, and closing it
  would reject standard VC properties this schema does not list.

IMPORTANT: This version is for W3C VC documents only. If you are NOT using the
W3C VC format (e.g. OpenAttestation v2 / OpenCerts verifiable documents), please
use an older version instead: transcripts/2.2 (or 1.0).
